# Evidence fixtures must retain their captured bytes across platforms. Any line-ending
# conversion would change the artifact and break the digests recorded in
# manifests/sources/*.json, which the fixture tests assert byte for byte.
fixtures/** -text
# The directory's own prose is documentation, not an artifact, so it is normalised.
fixtures/README.md text eol=lf

# Compilation artifacts are content-addressed the same way: an approved control pins one by
# SHA-256 and the resolver hashes the file's bytes to prove it is the artifact named.
#
# These particular artifacts are single-line canonical JSON and so contain no newlines to
# convert — the rule is here because the next artifact format may, and content whose digest
# is load-bearing should never be subject to translation in the first place.
data/compilations/** -text

# The frozen approval ledger beside the tests is the same kind of object, and was not covered
# here until it disagreed with reality. `core.autocrlf` is true on the authoring machine, so
# this file was checked out with LF while the ledger the live sequence-1 report was signed
# under held CRLF — identical text, different bytes, different digest. Tests threading it into
# a digest-checked boundary passed only because they compared it against itself.
tests/historical_pack.json -text

# The published bundles the site serves for download. `/verify` prints the sha256 of this
# exact file and tells a reader to check it, so the bytes are the claim. Without this rule the
# working tree held CRLF (sha 914ea892…) while the committed blob held LF (sha 0b9be60b…), and
# a deploy from a fresh clone would have served a file contradicting the hash printed beside
# its own download link.
site/data/** -text
